  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study applies structured jailbreak prompts and measures harmful-output success rates, testing prompt-based safety bypass with a different method. It does not test the proposed first-token mechanism or the specific prefix, suffix, or decoding interventions.",
    "gate_note": "The harmful-output ASR results for MultiCodeAttack are included because they measure harmful compliance under an attack prompt. The study does not measure aligned-versus-base per-token KL, fine-tuning on harmful or benign examples, compliant-opening prefills, adversarial suffixes as such, decoding changes, safety localization in weights, or safety deepening. Its descriptions of cited studies are not recorded as results.",
    "test_reason": "The study builds a code-template jailbreak and reports only its own attack success rates. V4 names prefilled compliant openings, suffixes and decoding changes, which are first-token bypasses. A natural-language code-template prompt is none of these. The paper has no fine-tuning, KL, localization or deepening results. Its high ASR shows prompt-based jailbreaks work, but that is a different construct from V4 and does not bear on whether getting past the opening defeats refusal.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is right. The authors present MultiCodeAttack as an iterative multi-language code-template jailbreak with high ASR across diverse LLMs, robust to several defenses, and as evidence of weaknesses in current alignment and defenses. It makes no claim about shallow safety alignment.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study proposes and evaluates an iterative multi-language code-template jailbreak, rather than fine-tuning, token-position KL measurement, or prefix, suffix, or decoding interventions.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The study reports variation by model, task, programming language/template, defense, number of attack rounds, and number of variants per round.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "An iterative multi-language code-template jailbreak reaches high ASR (about 93% harmful text, 92.5% malicious code) on 19 aligned LLMs, including o1-mini and o3-mini, and mostly keeps working under paraphrase, retokenization and perturbation defenses.",
        "proposed_claim": "Aligned LLMs remain vulnerable to structured code-template prompt jailbreaks (a prompt-level bypass, distinct from prefill, suffix or decoding attacks).",
